      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The corridor once housed legendary jazz venues like the Lincoln Theatre (1922) and Bohemian Caverns (1926), drawing artists such as Duke Ellington.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U_Street_(Washington%2C_D.C.)",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the 1968 riots, redevelopment projects opened the U Street station in 1991 and the Reeves Center in 1986, reshaping the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U_Street_(Washington%2C_D.C.)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Ben’s Chili Bowl, opened in 1958, remains a cultural landmark where generations have gathered since the mid‑20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://historicblackwallstreet.com/u-street-shaw-dc-black-wall-street-history-and-legacy/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1998 historic designation protected U Street’s architecture and helped preserve its legacy as a center of African‑American culture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://historicblackwallstreet.com/u-street-shaw-dc-black-wall-street-history-and-legacy/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The corridor’s roots trace back to post‑Civil War freedmen who settled near Camp Barker, sparking a vibrant community that grew with streetcar lines.",
